course: dictionary
lesson: 9357
title: A word that sounds like its meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound like the action they describe. Say splutter aloud and it spits and stumbles, just as the speaker does. Use the sound as a clue, then check the words around it.
explain: «The water is freezing; the Mole sinks, comes up spluttering, sinks again — until a firm paw grabs his neck.» The Mole is in trouble in the water, so spluttering must mean coughing and spitting out water, not talking.
explain: Splutter can also mean speaking in a broken, messy way when you are angry or shocked. «Spluttered with laughter» shows that laughing can make you choke and speak in bursts too. An engine can splutter as well.
fact: To splutter means to make spitting sounds, to cough noisily, or to make short, uneven popping noises.
fact: Splutter can be a verb («he spluttered») or a noun («a splutter»).
fact: «Spluttered» often shows a character is shocked, furious or embarrassed and cannot speak smoothly.
fact: «Splutter into life» describes an engine that starts with uneven popping noises.
fact: Words that sound like the thing they describe are a useful clue, but always check the context too.
exercise: «The Mole sinks, comes up spluttering, sinks again.» What is the Mole doing? | *Coughing and spitting out water | Swimming in a race | Sleeping on the riverbank | Singing a happy song
exercise: «The Otter hugs the Rat so hard he splutters through his buttered toast.» What does splutters mean here? | Sings a loud song | Falls fast asleep | Eats very neatly and quietly | *Speaks or coughs in a messy, spitting way
exercise: «The old car began to splutter into life on the cold morning.» What does this suggest? | It goes faster than a jet | It starts smoothly and quietly | *It starts with uneven popping noises | It never starts at all
test: «She let out a splutter of laughter.» Which word is the noun? | let | she | of | *splutter
test: Why might a person splutter? | *They are shocked or angry and cannot speak smoothly | They are sleeping soundly | They are reading a quiet book | They are counting slowly to ten
exam: «He spluttered with laughter at the joke.» Which meaning fits best? | *Laughed in a noisy, broken way, almost choking | Ran away quickly | Read the joke slowly | Stayed silent and still
